WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.327 --> 00:00:04.549
- Good morning Believe Nation,

00:00:04.549 --> 00:00:05.382
today we're going to talk

00:00:05.382 --> 00:00:08.104
about how you can study efficiently.

00:00:08.104 --> 00:00:09.670
Rule number three is my personal favorite,

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.776
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:11.776 --> 00:00:13.507
And as always guys as your listening,

00:00:13.507 --> 00:00:15.120
if you hear something that
really, really resonates

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.298
with you, please leave
it in the comments below

00:00:17.298 --> 00:00:18.805
and put quotes around it so other people

00:00:18.805 --> 00:00:21.469
can be inspired and also
as you write things down

00:00:21.469 --> 00:00:26.167
you're much more likely to
keep it in here as well.

00:00:26.167 --> 00:00:27.488
Enjoy.

00:00:27.488 --> 00:00:30.821
(upbeat tranquil music)

00:00:44.502 --> 00:00:45.995
- And you may have heard
of the Pomodoro technique,

00:00:45.995 --> 00:00:48.255
everyone talks about it,
but in case you haven't,

00:00:48.255 --> 00:00:50.710
it's simply a technique where
you set a timer for about

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:54.279
25 minutes, classically, and
then you work only on one

00:00:54.279 --> 00:00:57.059
task during that 25 minutes session.

00:00:57.059 --> 00:00:59.408
And I think a lot of people
do this, and it's very useful,

00:00:59.408 --> 00:01:01.801
but there are some areas for
improvement that I don't think

00:01:01.801 --> 00:01:03.507
a lot of people take advantage of.

00:01:03.507 --> 00:01:05.442
So let me just rapid
fire give them to you.

00:01:05.442 --> 00:01:07.955
So number one and I talked
about how much I'm a fan

00:01:07.955 --> 00:01:09.356
of the Beeminder app.

00:01:09.356 --> 00:01:12.523
The Beeminder blog is also a
good resource for productivity

00:01:12.523 --> 00:01:14.972
techniques and experiments.

00:01:14.972 --> 00:01:16.877
And one of the things
that they talk about,

00:01:16.877 --> 00:01:18.716
is this thing they do called Tocks.

00:01:18.716 --> 00:01:21.129
Well a tock is essentially
a Pomodoro session

00:01:21.129 --> 00:01:23.705
except for they use about 45 minutes,

00:01:23.705 --> 00:01:26.721
and then take 15 minutes
breaks instead of the classic

00:01:26.721 --> 00:01:28.638
25 minutes, 5 minute break structure.

00:01:28.638 --> 00:01:32.165
So the tip here is to experiment
with the time intervals.

00:01:32.165 --> 00:01:34.660
Don't just set yourself
to 25 minutes and assume

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.821
that that's the only
potential interval that

00:01:37.821 --> 00:01:38.842
you can study at.

00:01:38.842 --> 00:01:39.991
Find what works for you.

00:01:39.991 --> 00:01:41.808
And the other one,

00:01:41.808 --> 00:01:43.357
is to put a piece of paper next to you,

00:01:43.357 --> 00:01:45.569
during your Pomodoro session
and whenever anything

00:01:45.569 --> 00:01:47.043
comes up that distracts you,

00:01:47.043 --> 00:01:48.934
maybe a phone call or the
urge to check Facebook

00:01:48.934 --> 00:01:50.606
or something, write it down.

00:01:50.606 --> 00:01:52.320
Now this let's you do two things.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.034
One, you can remember
what the distraction was

00:01:54.034 --> 00:01:55.929
and if it happened to be something urgent,

00:01:55.929 --> 00:01:58.139
you can take care of it
during your break time.

00:01:58.139 --> 00:02:01.129
But two, as you continue to
do lots of Pomodoro sessions

00:02:01.129 --> 00:02:03.967
over months and months,
you start to see what are

00:02:03.967 --> 00:02:06.394
the common problem points.

00:02:06.394 --> 00:02:08.203
What comes up a lot that distracts you,

00:02:08.203 --> 00:02:10.338
and then you can take steps
to prevent these things.

00:02:10.338 --> 00:02:11.669
So maybe if it's your phone,

00:02:11.669 --> 00:02:13.522
you forget to put it
in Do Not Disturb Mode.

00:02:13.522 --> 00:02:14.542
Well you can do that now.

00:02:14.542 --> 00:02:16.830
If it's a certain website
that you really want to visit

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:19.150
because it's so distracting
and draws you in,

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:22.126
then you can use an extension
like StayFocusd on Chrome

00:02:22.126 --> 00:02:23.786
to block it during your study sessions.

00:02:23.786 --> 00:02:24.892
Very useful stuff.

00:02:24.892 --> 00:02:27.564
- You have to schedule your time.

00:02:27.564 --> 00:02:31.735
Don't just stuff studying in
to cracks in your schedule.

00:02:31.735 --> 00:02:33.985
Your schedule should be
built around studying.

00:02:33.985 --> 00:02:34.818
What does this mean?

00:02:34.818 --> 00:02:37.117
This means have an exact
set time during the day

00:02:37.117 --> 00:02:38.292
when you start to study.

00:02:38.292 --> 00:02:41.084
For me I remember, it was about 6 p.m.

00:02:41.084 --> 00:02:43.122
I would always to myself, okay, 6 p.m.

00:02:43.122 --> 00:02:46.214
I get back from school,
I rest a little bit,

00:02:46.214 --> 00:02:48.287
and then at 6 p.m. that's my study time.

00:02:48.287 --> 00:02:49.315
It starts.

00:02:49.315 --> 00:02:50.379
How long does it go?

00:02:50.379 --> 00:02:52.249
Well that depends on how much assignments

00:02:52.249 --> 00:02:53.712
and homework I have.

00:02:53.712 --> 00:02:57.215
But it would go for at
least, an hour or two,

00:02:57.215 --> 00:03:00.682
and sometimes it would go
for four, five, or six hours.

00:03:00.682 --> 00:03:03.391
And it would start at six,
and it would usually end like,

00:03:03.391 --> 00:03:06.612
around nine, or 10, or after that.

00:03:06.612 --> 00:03:07.486
And it would vary.

00:03:07.486 --> 00:03:09.160
But I think what's really
important is to have that

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:10.138
starting time.

00:03:10.138 --> 00:03:10.971
Six.

00:03:10.971 --> 00:03:11.804
No matter what.

00:03:11.804 --> 00:03:12.637
Six.

00:03:12.637 --> 00:03:13.470
Sit down and study.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.297
Open the books, sit down, study.

00:03:15.297 --> 00:03:16.938
It doesn't have to be six for you.

00:03:16.938 --> 00:03:18.326
Just come up with your own time.

00:03:18.326 --> 00:03:20.265
And, you're doing this every single day.

00:03:20.265 --> 00:03:21.621
What's important about this,

00:03:21.621 --> 00:03:23.679
is that it leads us to the next point.

00:03:23.679 --> 00:03:24.767
Which is you have to get comfortable

00:03:24.767 --> 00:03:26.363
and you have to slow yourself down

00:03:26.363 --> 00:03:28.118
in order to study effectively.

00:03:28.118 --> 00:03:30.897
You can't rush the studying process.

00:03:30.897 --> 00:03:34.094
And one of the secrets that
I use, is simply Brute Force.

00:03:34.094 --> 00:03:36.909
Right, a lot of the issues as
a student that I dealt with,

00:03:36.909 --> 00:03:38.361
the things that I couldn't understand,

00:03:38.361 --> 00:03:41.028
I just Brute Forced
them through repetition.

00:03:41.028 --> 00:03:42.697
And this repetition can take time.

00:03:42.697 --> 00:03:44.585
Also, you know you have to
be patient with yourself.

00:03:44.585 --> 00:03:46.211
Sometimes you're not going
to understand the problem

00:03:46.211 --> 00:03:47.395
right off the bat.

00:03:47.395 --> 00:03:48.481
So you just kind of repeat it,

00:03:48.481 --> 00:03:50.332
repeat it, mull it over, mull it over,

00:03:50.332 --> 00:03:52.684
and that means sometimes,
it takes you longer.

00:03:52.684 --> 00:03:54.627
So instead of finishing a
quick little math assignment

00:03:54.627 --> 00:03:56.999
in hour, it might take you three hours.

00:03:56.999 --> 00:04:01.263
But that's worth it, if
you calm yourself down,

00:04:01.263 --> 00:04:03.286
get comfortable, slow
yourself down, kind of,

00:04:03.286 --> 00:04:05.636
open yourself to absorbing
this information.

00:04:05.636 --> 00:04:07.935
Right, this is where the love it comes in.

00:04:07.935 --> 00:04:09.423
If you don't love it,
and what you're doing

00:04:09.423 --> 00:04:11.235
is you're just kind of
rush studying through stuff

00:04:11.235 --> 00:04:13.266
just to learn it, like
cramming for an exam.

00:04:13.266 --> 00:04:15.116
That's just horrible.

00:04:15.116 --> 00:04:15.955
That's horrible.

00:04:15.955 --> 00:04:17.615
Cause all you're trying
to do is you're jumping,

00:04:17.615 --> 00:04:19.141
you're like a dog jumping through hoops.

00:04:19.141 --> 00:04:21.071
That's basically what you
are when you're doing that.

00:04:21.071 --> 00:04:22.558
Because, you're saying to yourself,

00:04:22.558 --> 00:04:24.478
well I got to pass this exam
in order to get into college

00:04:24.478 --> 00:04:27.483
to get good grades so my parents like me.

00:04:27.483 --> 00:04:29.235
That's just hoops like a
dog you're jumping through.

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:30.241
Don't do that.

00:04:30.241 --> 00:04:32.109
Instead, what you think
about, is like this,

00:04:32.109 --> 00:04:34.818
okay this subject matter
is really fascinating,

00:04:34.818 --> 00:04:35.960
let me study more.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:37.627
Let me learn more, I want to
know how math interconnects

00:04:37.627 --> 00:04:39.040
with, with history.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.866
And I want to know how history
interconnects with science.

00:04:40.866 --> 00:04:42.669
And I want to know how
this and this connect,

00:04:42.669 --> 00:04:45.104
and I want to know more
depth about this thing here.

00:04:45.104 --> 00:04:46.907
That's how you got to think.

00:04:46.907 --> 00:04:49.416
- My general process is,

00:04:49.416 --> 00:04:51.833
you can think of it as, DSSS,

00:04:53.366 --> 00:04:55.033
so D, S, S, S, okay.

00:04:56.279 --> 00:04:59.040
And the first step is, deconstruction,

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.707
and that entails taking learn X.

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:05.509
Which is generally very
large and nebulous,

00:05:05.509 --> 00:05:06.574
learn Spanish.

00:05:06.574 --> 00:05:09.191
And breaking it down into component parts

00:05:09.191 --> 00:05:10.554
and making it a measurable goal,

00:05:10.554 --> 00:05:11.966
as opposed to just, learn.

00:05:11.966 --> 00:05:13.414
Because that doesn't have an end point,

00:05:13.414 --> 00:05:14.768
and what that means, with no end point,

00:05:14.768 --> 00:05:18.060
is that you can't celebrate
passing certain milestones.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:19.936
So first is kind of deciding on,

00:05:19.936 --> 00:05:22.218
exactly what you're trying to accomplish,

00:05:22.218 --> 00:05:24.836
and then, asking a set of questions.

00:05:24.836 --> 00:05:27.804
Either to Google and or to experts.

00:05:27.804 --> 00:05:30.279
Such as, who's good at
this who shouldn't be?

00:05:30.279 --> 00:05:32.055
That's a very important one.

00:05:32.055 --> 00:05:33.512
So if I'm looking at say,

00:05:33.512 --> 00:05:34.882
Ultra Endurance Running,

00:05:34.882 --> 00:05:36.378
I would say, who's good
at this who shouldn't be?

00:05:36.378 --> 00:05:39.278
Meaning, the people who are
already built like spiders,

00:05:39.278 --> 00:05:42.110
they're genetically predisposed
to be very good at this.

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:45.390
So they might be succeeding
despite bad training habits.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:48.560
I want to find the person
who has, bad attributes,

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:49.983
like somebody who's 240 pounds.

00:05:49.983 --> 00:05:51.156
- Like me.
- Yeah. (Chuckling)

00:05:51.156 --> 00:05:53.042
Although you got the Soccer.

00:05:53.042 --> 00:05:55.107
But it's like you could
find somebody who's,

00:05:55.107 --> 00:05:57.485
and I did, somebody who's
like 230, 240 pounds,

00:05:57.485 --> 00:05:59.359
really big, maybe a little chunky

00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:01.361
and they still run 50 hundred mile races.

00:06:01.361 --> 00:06:04.543
That means, most likely,
that they are compensating

00:06:04.543 --> 00:06:06.239
for bad attributes for good training.

00:06:06.239 --> 00:06:07.756
I would say, who are the best coaches

00:06:07.756 --> 00:06:09.531
and who are the most
controversial coaches.

00:06:09.531 --> 00:06:10.798
- Oh, controversy.

00:06:10.798 --> 00:06:13.358
- And look at the training
methodologies of the coaches

00:06:13.358 --> 00:06:15.552
and the most important
thing there, is not,

00:06:15.552 --> 00:06:17.494
was the coach formally
extremely successful

00:06:17.494 --> 00:06:19.307
in their chosen sport,
although it's helpful.

00:06:19.307 --> 00:06:21.518
Were they able to replicate their results?

00:06:21.518 --> 00:06:22.510
- Great, so I've lost track,

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:23.440
are we in the D still?

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:24.273
- We're still in D.

00:06:24.273 --> 00:06:25.546
- Okay, D, we're deconstructing.

00:06:25.546 --> 00:06:26.625
- Yep, so deconstructing,

00:06:26.625 --> 00:06:28.274
that's I would say the most important

00:06:28.274 --> 00:06:30.392
information gathering phase.

00:06:30.392 --> 00:06:32.081
The next is, selection.

00:06:32.081 --> 00:06:33.641
And that's doing an 80/20 analysis.

00:06:33.641 --> 00:06:37.682
So, looking at the many
different ways you can

00:06:37.682 --> 00:06:39.780
train for your goal, which
you've already defined,

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:42.322
or practiced for it, say with Spanish.

00:06:42.322 --> 00:06:44.023
Like one on one lessons versus text book,

00:06:44.023 --> 00:06:45.423
versus this, versus that,
versus the other thing,

00:06:45.423 --> 00:06:47.150
versus vocabulary memorization.

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:51.804
What are the 20% that are
going to get me 80% to my goal?

00:06:51.804 --> 00:06:53.727
What are the, what are the critical few?

00:06:53.727 --> 00:06:55.650
And, then focusing on those.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:59.629
So you would, do this selection process.

00:06:59.629 --> 00:07:01.861
And very often, let's
say language, it's the,

00:07:01.861 --> 00:07:03.115
highest frequency words.

00:07:03.115 --> 00:07:04.843
You don't have to learn,
- The top 1000 words.

00:07:04.843 --> 00:07:05.864
- Yeah, top 1000 words

00:07:05.864 --> 00:07:08.881
are going to get you through
75%, 80% of any conversation.

00:07:08.881 --> 00:07:10.964
Then you have sequencing.

00:07:11.809 --> 00:07:12.868
Putting things in the right order.

00:07:12.868 --> 00:07:15.802
This is I think, the most
neglected secret sauce.

00:07:15.802 --> 00:07:18.666
It's like, how do you
take, what you've decided,

00:07:18.666 --> 00:07:21.131
what you've gathered and put
it into a logical progression?

00:07:21.131 --> 00:07:23.277
This is where things often fail.

00:07:23.277 --> 00:07:24.634
Really,
- Like this first,

00:07:24.634 --> 00:07:26.236
then this, then this.
- Yep, like, like

00:07:26.236 --> 00:07:27.584
for each skill asking yourself,

00:07:27.584 --> 00:07:29.038
like what are the
prerequisites to this skill?

00:07:29.038 --> 00:07:30.815
Alright, I'm doing that
right now in gymnastics.

00:07:30.815 --> 00:07:34.249
So like, for a press handstand, or for a,

00:07:34.249 --> 00:07:36.392
like a planche, it's a
little easier to describe.

00:07:36.392 --> 00:07:38.108
So planche is like a pushup on the ground,

00:07:38.108 --> 00:07:41.255
you lean forward so your
shoulders are way out here,

00:07:41.255 --> 00:07:43.112
and then you lift your
feet off the ground.

00:07:43.112 --> 00:07:43.945
Okay.

00:07:43.945 --> 00:07:46.304
So what are the
prerequisites to a planche?

00:07:46.304 --> 00:07:47.754
Like for people who can't do it.

00:07:47.754 --> 00:07:48.848
What are the prereqs?

00:07:48.848 --> 00:07:49.818
And, if someone says,

00:07:49.818 --> 00:07:51.416
"Well today we're going to practice this."

00:07:51.416 --> 00:07:53.286
Well what should I be
able to do before this?

00:07:53.286 --> 00:07:55.238
Technically, and physically.

00:07:55.238 --> 00:07:56.861
Maybe even emotionally right,

00:07:56.861 --> 00:07:58.008
if you're trying to learn how to be an EMT

00:07:58.008 --> 00:07:59.086
or something like that.

00:07:59.086 --> 00:08:02.806
Okay, that's how you piece
together this sequence.

00:08:02.806 --> 00:08:04.309
I spend a lot of time on the sequencing.

00:08:04.309 --> 00:08:06.568
But this is the exact order that I follow.

00:08:06.568 --> 00:08:09.070
And the last one, which is
almost universally neglected,

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:10.759
- The third S.
- The third S

00:08:10.759 --> 00:08:11.911
is steaks.

00:08:11.911 --> 00:08:14.011
Not steaks like on a grill,
but stakes like through

00:08:14.011 --> 00:08:15.458
a vampire chest.

00:08:15.458 --> 00:08:17.024
Meaning, consequences.

00:08:17.024 --> 00:08:18.933
You need to built in incentives.

00:08:18.933 --> 00:08:20.923
Because for most of us
who are trying to learn

00:08:20.923 --> 00:08:23.108
something, not always.

00:08:23.108 --> 00:08:24.925
These are extra-curriculars.

00:08:24.925 --> 00:08:26.802
Right, if you're trying to learn something

00:08:26.802 --> 00:08:29.233
for your job, the incentive
is, don't get fired.

00:08:29.233 --> 00:08:30.281
Or get a raise right.

00:08:30.281 --> 00:08:31.742
You have built in incentives.

00:08:31.742 --> 00:08:34.643
But for, I'm going to learn,

00:08:34.643 --> 00:08:36.081
fill in the blank.

00:08:36.081 --> 00:08:37.872
Right I'm going to
learn how to play tennis

00:08:37.872 --> 00:08:38.942
on my spare time.

00:08:38.942 --> 00:08:40.507
Or try to learn a little
bit of french before

00:08:40.507 --> 00:08:42.161
I go to France like six months from now.

00:08:42.161 --> 00:08:43.665
Those are extra-curriculars and they will

00:08:43.665 --> 00:08:45.069
get crowded out of your life,

00:08:45.069 --> 00:08:47.801
if you don't build in incentives.

00:08:47.801 --> 00:08:49.319
Meaning, rewards or punishments.

00:08:49.319 --> 00:08:51.850
So you can certainly read all about this,

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:53.248
in The 4-hour Chef, but the basic idea,

00:08:53.248 --> 00:08:55.009
I'll give you one example, would be,

00:08:55.009 --> 00:08:56.098
a betting pool.

00:08:56.098 --> 00:08:57.576
That could be with, like a friend,

00:08:57.576 --> 00:08:58.465
where if you miss a workout,

00:08:58.465 --> 00:09:00.653
you have to pay your friend
a certain amount of money.

00:09:00.653 --> 00:09:02.139
It could be a dollar, it could be $5.

00:09:02.139 --> 00:09:03.013
- [Man] Could be a hundred.

00:09:03.013 --> 00:09:04.866
- I know someone who works at Google,

00:09:04.866 --> 00:09:07.958
makes a lot of money,
who had an arrangement

00:09:07.958 --> 00:09:09.080
with his training partner,

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:12.011
they pay each other a dollar
if they missed a workout.

00:09:12.011 --> 00:09:14.762
And that was the first time
he stuck with his workout,

00:09:14.762 --> 00:09:17.126
lost a hundred pounds.

00:09:17.126 --> 00:09:18.298
- Wow, a buck.

00:09:18.298 --> 00:09:20.876
- So the amount can have
a disproportionate impact.

00:09:20.876 --> 00:09:22.916
But I also know, people for instance,

00:09:22.916 --> 00:09:24.408
who get together four or five friends,

00:09:24.408 --> 00:09:25.951
and they'll have a
competition like who loses

00:09:25.951 --> 00:09:28.578
the most body fat, by x point in time,

00:09:28.578 --> 00:09:29.411
eight weeks from now.

00:09:29.411 --> 00:09:31.760
Great, you want to learn
how to exercise properly,

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:34.800
with the goal of losing fat,
put some money on the line.

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:38.559
You will work infinitely
harder to not lost $100,

00:09:38.559 --> 00:09:40.324
than you will to make $100.

00:09:40.324 --> 00:09:42.877
So, the stakes I think is,

00:09:42.877 --> 00:09:45.150
the consequences I think
are very important.

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:46.933
You can find say, accountability coaches

00:09:46.933 --> 00:09:49.028
through places like stickk.com,

00:09:49.028 --> 00:09:51.716
s, t, i, c, k, k, .com, coach.me.

00:09:51.716 --> 00:09:53.557
Which I actually, I have
some involvement with.

00:09:53.557 --> 00:09:55.229
And others.

00:09:55.229 --> 00:09:57.104
But you need, incentives.

00:09:57.104 --> 00:09:59.959
You're only going to be
as faithful to your goals,

00:09:59.959 --> 00:10:01.991
are your incentives compel you to be.

00:10:01.991 --> 00:10:03.239
That's it.

00:10:03.239 --> 00:10:04.072
- Sweet.

00:10:04.072 --> 00:10:05.751
So let's, quick recap.

00:10:05.751 --> 00:10:06.938
- Deconstruction.

00:10:06.938 --> 00:10:08.175
- Deconstruct. First S?

00:10:08.175 --> 00:10:09.354
- Selection, 80/20.

00:10:09.354 --> 00:10:10.833
- Second S?
- Which is 80/20 analysis,

00:10:10.833 --> 00:10:13.833
then the second S is, is sequencing.

00:10:15.846 --> 00:10:16.794
Putting things in the right order.

00:10:16.794 --> 00:10:18.763
And the last S, is stakes.

00:10:18.763 --> 00:10:19.871
- Stakes, there you go.

00:10:19.871 --> 00:10:20.774
DSSS it.

00:10:20.774 --> 00:10:21.967
- DSSS.

00:10:21.967 --> 00:10:23.606
- Step number four is, clear your mind

00:10:23.606 --> 00:10:25.172
of all distractions.

00:10:25.172 --> 00:10:27.010
I used to hold the record for the fastest

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:29.328
to memorize a deck of
cards in the United States.

00:10:29.328 --> 00:10:31.936
And sometimes before I would
memorize a deck of cards,

00:10:31.936 --> 00:10:34.239
my brain would be worried
about things in my life,

00:10:34.239 --> 00:10:35.513
or things that were goin on.

00:10:35.513 --> 00:10:38.490
So what I would do to clear
my mind of all distractions,

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:39.883
is I would close my eyes,

00:10:39.883 --> 00:10:41.774
and I would visualize those scenarios

00:10:41.774 --> 00:10:43.490
the way that I wished they were.

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:45.032
Or the way that I hoped
they would be one day,

00:10:45.032 --> 00:10:48.390
then I would open my
eyes, I would be relieved

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:49.497
of that stress,

00:10:49.497 --> 00:10:51.103
and then I would memorize a deck of cards.

00:10:51.103 --> 00:10:53.382
Clear your mind of all distractions.

00:10:53.382 --> 00:10:56.221
Maybe close your eyes,
and visualize the things

00:10:56.221 --> 00:10:57.497
the way you wished they could be,

00:10:57.497 --> 00:11:00.243
and then focus on your studying.

00:11:00.243 --> 00:11:01.621
- A lot of people just jump in this,

00:11:01.621 --> 00:11:03.287
like oh I want to learn faster,
I want to learn speed reading,

00:11:03.287 --> 00:11:05.502
I want to learn, you know a new language,

00:11:05.502 --> 00:11:06.977
I want to learn all these new skills,

00:11:06.977 --> 00:11:08.057
I want to learn all these things.

00:11:08.057 --> 00:11:09.939
And what is happening,
people are very transient

00:11:09.939 --> 00:11:11.438
with their learning goals.

00:11:11.438 --> 00:11:12.853
What do I mean by that?

00:11:12.853 --> 00:11:14.784
I mean, they're kind of phony about it.

00:11:14.784 --> 00:11:16.091
They really don't want to learn.

00:11:16.091 --> 00:11:17.181
It's I'd like to learn this,

00:11:17.181 --> 00:11:18.014
I'd like to learn that.

00:11:18.014 --> 00:11:19.035
Sometime I'd like to learn this,

00:11:19.035 --> 00:11:20.647
someday I'd like to learn french.

00:11:20.647 --> 00:11:22.662
Someday I'd like to
learn how to do photos.

00:11:22.662 --> 00:11:24.384
Someday's I'd like to blog,

00:11:24.384 --> 00:11:25.591
someday I'd like to do video,

00:11:25.591 --> 00:11:27.444
and they never actually learn it,

00:11:27.444 --> 00:11:29.824
because learning doesn't happen someday.

00:11:29.824 --> 00:11:31.687
It has to happen, consistently.

00:11:31.687 --> 00:11:33.286
And that's the thing.

00:11:33.286 --> 00:11:35.094
If we're really going to get the point

00:11:35.094 --> 00:11:37.729
where you learn something
faster, we have to approach

00:11:37.729 --> 00:11:39.732
learning itself differently.

00:11:39.732 --> 00:11:42.082
It can't be these one off little goals,

00:11:42.082 --> 00:11:43.569
of little things we want to learn,

00:11:43.569 --> 00:11:45.395
that doesn't work for people.

00:11:45.395 --> 00:11:47.270
They don't stay consistent
just like people

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:49.640
don't stay consistent on a diet,

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:51.695
if just their goal is to lose five pounds.

00:11:51.695 --> 00:11:54.505
There has to be a broader vision

00:11:54.505 --> 00:11:57.842
for who they are and what
they want to be about.

00:11:57.842 --> 00:11:59.838
And what they want their life to be about.

00:11:59.838 --> 00:12:01.297
If they're going to stay on a diet,

00:12:01.297 --> 00:12:02.742
same thing for learning.

00:12:02.742 --> 00:12:05.265
If you're going to stay
on a learning habit,

00:12:05.265 --> 00:12:07.910
then you have to have a
bigger vision for yourself.

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:09.375
So let's start with this conversation.

00:12:09.375 --> 00:12:11.340
If you want to learn something faster,

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:13.673
you must first and foremost,

00:12:14.816 --> 00:12:17.166
know the connection between
what you want to learn

00:12:17.166 --> 00:12:19.611
and your dream life.

00:12:19.611 --> 00:12:21.832
Your real lifestyle that you want to have.

00:12:21.832 --> 00:12:24.200
I tell people all the time,
don't just come up with

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:25.706
things you want to
learn, cause guess what?

00:12:25.706 --> 00:12:26.539
We want to learn everything.

00:12:26.539 --> 00:12:29.621
By human build, we are curious animals.

00:12:29.621 --> 00:12:31.402
We're deathly curious,

00:12:31.402 --> 00:12:33.958
I mean we are literally the
curiosity killed the cat,

00:12:33.958 --> 00:12:35.808
sort of society right now.

00:12:35.808 --> 00:12:38.681
Everyone wants to learn
50 new things immediately

00:12:38.681 --> 00:12:40.006
but no one ever learns anything, why?

00:12:40.006 --> 00:12:41.716
Because it's not real for them,

00:12:41.716 --> 00:12:43.787
it's a transient desire.

00:12:43.787 --> 00:12:45.720
If you want to become a great learner,

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:48.771
it has to be attached to
a dream life of yours.

00:12:48.771 --> 00:12:52.740
A true, a true lifestyle
that you see for yourself.

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:54.249
Let me give you an example,
lots of people say,

00:12:54.249 --> 00:12:55.855
"I'd like to learn some french.

00:12:55.855 --> 00:12:57.223
Ah, someday I'm going to learn french.

00:12:57.223 --> 00:12:58.352
Someday I'll learn how to talk french

00:12:58.352 --> 00:12:59.528
because it's a romantic language,

00:12:59.528 --> 00:13:01.318
I like the food and they
give lots of allowances

00:13:01.318 --> 00:13:02.901
on cheese and wine, I like that."

00:13:02.901 --> 00:13:03.962
Right and so,

00:13:03.962 --> 00:13:05.291
I'm french I can say a
little bit of that okay.

00:13:05.291 --> 00:13:07.454
So, I have a little piece of french in me

00:13:07.454 --> 00:13:08.421
I should say.

00:13:08.421 --> 00:13:09.943
So here's what's happening,

00:13:09.943 --> 00:13:12.462
if I don't have the vision, for myself,

00:13:12.462 --> 00:13:15.518
someday going to experience France,

00:13:15.518 --> 00:13:17.982
or someday going to a place
where they speak french,

00:13:17.982 --> 00:13:19.847
or someday having real interactions

00:13:19.847 --> 00:13:22.046
with a human being, speaking french,

00:13:22.046 --> 00:13:24.686
and it's important to me.

00:13:24.686 --> 00:13:27.200
And is a deep, sort of dream, desire,

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:28.255
vision for that.

00:13:28.255 --> 00:13:30.133
I'm never going to do the work

00:13:30.133 --> 00:13:32.459
to actually learn french.

00:13:32.459 --> 00:13:34.292
There's no why, there.

00:13:35.270 --> 00:13:38.354
You have to know what
you want of your life,

00:13:38.354 --> 00:13:40.460
before you become a master learner.

00:13:40.460 --> 00:13:41.840
Let me say that again.

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:44.375
You have to know what
you want of your life,

00:13:44.375 --> 00:13:46.487
before you become a master learner.

00:13:46.487 --> 00:13:48.769
Until I know what I want
my life to be about,

00:13:48.769 --> 00:13:50.772
what I want to experience
on a daily basis,

00:13:50.772 --> 00:13:52.263
what do I want my dreams to be,

00:13:52.263 --> 00:13:53.450
who I want to be,

00:13:53.450 --> 00:13:55.775
I don't know what to learn.

00:13:55.775 --> 00:13:57.927
I'm just going to be curious about things

00:13:57.927 --> 00:13:58.982
and I'll dabble in them.

00:13:58.982 --> 00:14:01.745
But that path to real true mastery,

00:14:01.745 --> 00:14:02.624
is that part where we say,

00:14:02.624 --> 00:14:04.868
okay what do I want my life to be about?

00:14:04.868 --> 00:14:07.690
Now let me develop the
knowledge, the skills,

00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:11.694
the competencies, the capabilities
to build into that life.

00:14:11.694 --> 00:14:13.555
And that's where you get someone

00:14:13.555 --> 00:14:14.716
who becomes a master learner.

00:14:14.716 --> 00:14:16.021
Now I'm not saying you have to know

00:14:16.021 --> 00:14:18.846
your ultimate purpose in life
before you can learn anything.

00:14:18.846 --> 00:14:20.529
I'm just saying if you
want to learn faster,

00:14:20.529 --> 00:14:22.515
start figuring out that purpose.

00:14:22.515 --> 00:14:24.160
Start figuring out your direction,

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:25.302
you'll learn a lot faster.

00:14:25.302 --> 00:14:27.977
- My favorite study hack
in university was to listen

00:14:27.977 --> 00:14:28.810
to music.

00:14:28.810 --> 00:14:30.739
Not just any music, specifically it was,

00:14:30.739 --> 00:14:33.114
the Phantom of the Opera,
instrumental soundtrack,

00:14:33.114 --> 00:14:35.397
and the Jurassic Park
instrumental soundtrack.

00:14:35.397 --> 00:14:37.752
And I listened to those,
over and over and over

00:14:37.752 --> 00:14:38.868
and over again.

00:14:38.868 --> 00:14:39.899
And I didn't know why.

00:14:39.899 --> 00:14:41.668
I just felt like it helped
me be more receptive

00:14:41.668 --> 00:14:42.503
to the information.

00:14:42.503 --> 00:14:43.999
I felt that I absorbed it more,

00:14:43.999 --> 00:14:45.254
and I could study better.

00:14:45.254 --> 00:14:46.957
And I just looped those
tracks, over and over

00:14:46.957 --> 00:14:48.684
and over and over and over again.

00:14:48.684 --> 00:14:51.338
Now, science has shown us
that listening to instrumental

00:14:51.338 --> 00:14:54.709
music, especially Mozart, if
you can handle Mozart music,

00:14:54.709 --> 00:14:56.421
that really can get you in the zone,

00:14:56.421 --> 00:14:58.739
and really open your
mind up and be receptive

00:14:58.739 --> 00:15:00.062
to information coming in.

00:15:00.062 --> 00:15:02.562
We've gone so far as to even
make playlists for studying

00:15:02.562 --> 00:15:04.922
that you can check out in our five series.

00:15:04.922 --> 00:15:06.703
And so, music I feel is really important.

00:15:06.703 --> 00:15:09.289
I think music sets the
tone for a lot of things,

00:15:09.289 --> 00:15:10.401
whatever you're trying to do in life.

00:15:10.401 --> 00:15:12.079
If you want to be motivated,

00:15:12.079 --> 00:15:13.927
you can listen to inspiring soundtracks.

00:15:13.927 --> 00:15:16.699
If you want to focus, you
can listen to instrumental,

00:15:16.699 --> 00:15:17.619
classical music.

00:15:17.619 --> 00:15:20.014
For studying especially,
I found that that really,

00:15:20.014 --> 00:15:21.200
really helped.

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:22.248
And so I would encourage you,

00:15:22.248 --> 00:15:25.618
to make a playlist that is
focused on instrumentals.

00:15:25.618 --> 00:15:27.077
You could try the ones
that we put together,

00:15:27.077 --> 00:15:28.489
or you can create your own.

00:15:28.489 --> 00:15:30.084
Just make sure that they're instrumental.

00:15:30.084 --> 00:15:31.464
There's no lyrics, because the lyrics

00:15:31.464 --> 00:15:32.595
can distract you.

00:15:32.595 --> 00:15:34.895
And that you're playing
the same songs repeatedly,

00:15:34.895 --> 00:15:36.459
over and over, and over and over again,

00:15:36.459 --> 00:15:39.186
so you don't get too distracted
and go somewhere else.

00:15:39.186 --> 00:15:40.685
So that was my biggest study hack

00:15:40.685 --> 00:15:42.136
that really helped me through university.

00:15:42.136 --> 00:15:44.469
And I hope it helps you too.

